    "The zero-flow value is an extrapolation far outside the 60-144 cm/s fit range.",
    "The gap numbers are our analysis of NASA's figures. The direction is NASA's own finding: in Figs. 7-9 \"the lunar-g buoyant limit was found to be less than the extrapolated value (for any assumed buoyant velocity) from the WSTF testing. This result is contrary to expected behavior\" (ICES-2025-392 p. 9).",
    "The images printed as Figs. 7-9 may be shifted by one material. Fitted to their points, Fig. 7 ('PMMA') matches Table 2's P 213 row, Fig. 8 ('P-213') its Beefy-T flaming row, and Fig. 9 repeats Fig. 10 (smolder). Fig. 7 also lacks the PMMA tests at 25 and 40 cm/s that p. 8 says are shown. We keep the captions' labels and flag this; we cannot confirm which material each image shows (ICES-2025-392 pp. 8, 11).",
    "The 1-g fit is assumed linear; real flammability curves often bend at low flow."
  ],
